we covered monitoring gke clusters all  the way back in our first episode  but there are lots of new features in  cloud operations to help you observe and  manage your gke clusters  and the workloads that run on them today  i'm going to show you how to use cloud  operations for gke  to make sure your services are healthy  this is a stackdoctor  [Music]  right away you'll see that the new gk  monitoring dashboard looks very  different  instead of three tabs you now have lists  of all the different gke entities  like clusters namespaces and nodes  remember this information is aggregated  from all the projects being monitored by  your workspace  this gives you a single place to see  everything you're responsible for  managing  now right now you might be thinking well  that's great but i have a default  namespace in every cluster  how will i ever find what i need well  there's a really cool filtering feature  that will help you to find exactly what  you're looking for  for example if you have a copy of the  front-end service running three  different clusters  the filter lets you select the exact one  you want once you apply the filter the  entire dashboard is filtered to match  one of the things that's been kind of  hard to do in the past is to get an  aggregated view of the data  for example what does my resource  utilization look like across my  namespace  this new view makes it really easy to  get this kind of aggregation  and it'll scale no matter how many  resources are being monitored even if  you have thousands of ponds across  hundreds of namespaces but if you do  want to see all of those resources you  can click view all  that will let you see all of the  entities in that category if that list  is too overwhelming you can filter from  here too  at any point you can select a row in the  table to get its details for example  click a pod to open its details panel  this view has everything you need in one  place  metadata about the resource any  incidents that it has triggered and  metric unlocked data too  having a single place to access all this  information makes understanding what's  going on  much easier on the metrics tab you can  easily select a smaller subset of time  if you're trying to zero in on an  anomaly just drag a rectangle on the  chart that includes the time you want to  focus on  from any metric chart you can then  create a learning policy by clicking on  that link  this is a great way to quickly get  started with alerting on gk entities  right in context you can also get right  to the logs for the resource you've  selected  for example select the pod and click on  the logs tab to see the most recent logs  for it  if you're troubleshooting an issue you  can filter the logs for example you  might only want to see  error logs to figure out why a pod is  crashing to get more details click on  the open  and logging button that will open the  new logs viewer with your query  pre-populated and executed  this really makes it very easy to know  exactly what's going on with your  workloads  without having to figure out how to  select the resource you're looking for  you may recall that we covered using  logging with your gt clusters and  workloads in an earlier episode  we'll link to that below if you haven't  seen it already thanks for joining me  today  we took a look at the new gke monitoring  dashboard and learn how to use its  in-context capabilities  to set up alerting and pull up logs for  our workload  thanks for watching and come back soon  for more this is a stackdoctor stay  healthy out there
 

